 r←CreateParms;⎕IO;⎕ML
⍝ Returns a namespace with default values for `Process`. Its niladic function `∆List` lists all parameters
⍝ with their current values.
⍝ | Parameter    | Description|
⍝ | - | - |
⍝ | `addToMsg`   | Empty by default. A text or a list of texts that is added to the log entry, the Windows event and the HTML report.|
⍝ | `checkErrorFolder` | Boolean that defaults to 1. If this is 1 and the folder `errorFolder` does **not** exist<br>it will be created. |
⍝ | `createHTML` | Boolean that defaults to 1. A 0 means that no HTML file will be created.|
⍝ | `customFns`  | Empty by default. Name of a monadic function to be executed by `Process`, with the parameter space as right argument. Useful to send an email, for example.<br>Either a fully qualified name like `#.MyApp.OnCrash`, or a simple name together with `customFnsParent`.|
⍝ | `customFnsParent` | No default. A reference to the parent of `customFns`, which must then be a simple name, without dots. Needed only in case the parent is a class instance.<br>Ignored when `customFns` contains a dot.|
⍝ | `enforceOff` | Boolean that defaults to 0. With a 1 `Process` acts in a development session as it does in a runtime: it `⎕OFF`s (unless `off` is 0 or `signal` is set), kills threads and prints nothing to the session. Mainly for test cases.|
⍝ | `errorFolder`| Folder that keeps the crash files: the HTML page, the component file with `crash`, the error WS and, with `stormGuard` 1, `StormGuard.txt`. See below for rules.<br>`Process` replaces the value by the full path of the folder.|
⍝ | `killThreads`| Boolean that defaults to 1, meaning that all threads that can be killed will be killed.<br>Has no effect in development unless `enforceOff` is 1: there the session's own threads would be killed as well.|
⍝ | `logFunction`| Empty by default. Name of a monadic function that is called several times per crash, with a text or a list of texts to be logged.<br>Named like `customFns`: either a fully qualified name, or a simple name together with `logFunctionParent`.|
⍝ | `logFunctionParent` | No default. A reference to the parent of `logFunction`, which must then be a simple name, without dots. Needed only in case the parent is a class instance.<br>Ignored when `logFunction` contains a dot.|
⍝ | `off`        | Boolean that defaults to 1 which makes `Process` execute `⎕OFF` with `returnCode`, but only in a runtime or with `enforceOff` 1.<br>With 0 `Process` just returns, and the function that failed does not continue. Ignored when `signal` is not 0.|
⍝ | `returnCode` | Defaults to 1. The return code passed on to `⎕OFF`.|
⍝ | `saveCrash`  | Boolean that defaults to 1.<br>A 0 suppresses the component file in which `crash` is saved.|
⍝ | `saveErrorWS`| Boolean that defaults to 1.<br>A 0 suppresses the creation of a crash workspace. There is no point in attempting<br>to save a WS in a multi-threaded application for example.|
⍝ | `saveVars`   | Boolean that defaults to 1.<br>If 1 the variables (no references) of the namespace the failing function was running in are saved in a<br>sub namespace `Vars` within `crash`, and with that in the component file and the error WS.|
⍝ | `signal`     | Defaults to 0. Any other number is `⎕SIGNAL`led by `Process` once the crash is recorded, instead of `⎕OFF`.<br>This can be used for a restart attempt, but the application must trap that number ahead of `Process`:<br>see "Carrying on after an error" in the documentation. Use a number between 500 and 999.|
⍝ | `stormCeiling` | Defaults to 10. With `stormGuard` 1, the maximum number of crashes within one hour that get the full treatment, across all signatures.|
⍝ | `stormGuard` | Boolean that defaults to 0. 1 switches on the guard against crash storms: a recurring error gets the full treatment<br>only at its 1st, 2nd, 4th, 8th... occurrence, and when its last one is `stormRepeatAfter` hours old; otherwise just the log entry and the Windows event are written.<br>Requires that only one process writes to the error folder.|
⍝ | `stormRepeatAfter` | Defaults to 24. With `stormGuard` 1, the number of hours after which an error that keeps occurring gets the full treatment again, whatever its count. 0 switches this off.|
⍝ | `stormResetAfter` | Defaults to 24. With `stormGuard` 1, the number of hours after which an error that has not occurred starts again at 1.|
⍝ | `trapInternalErrors` | Boolean that defaults to 1: all internal errors are trapped,<br>`Process` should never crash an application. Set it to 0 to investigate a problem within `HandleError`.|
⍝ | `trapSaveWSID`       | **Deprecated** since 5.0.3 and ignored: `trapInternalErrors` now governs the `⎕SAVE` statement as well.|
⍝ | `windowsEventSource` | Empty by default. Name of the Windows Event Source to write to. The source must exist: creating one requires admin rights.<br>Ignored when empty.|\\
⍝ Since version 2.2.0 `errorFolder` defaults to an empty vector. Assuming that the WS or the stand-alone EXE have the name "Foo" the following rules hold true:
⍝ * If `errorFolder` is empty then it defaults to "%LOCALAPPDATA%/Foo/Errors" under Windows and "Errors/" in the current
⍝   directory otherwise. In a clear workspace "ERROR_WS" takes the place of "Foo".
⍝ * If `errorFolder` is "Errors/" then this folder will be expected in the current directory.
⍝ * If `errorFolder` is an absolute path it is assumed that the last part is the folder that shall hold the crash files.\\
⍝ In all three instances if the folder in question does not exist but `checkErrorFolder` is 1 the folder will be created.
 ⎕IO←0 ⋄ ⎕ML←3
 r←⎕NS''
 r.⎕FX'r←∆List;⎕IO' '⍝ List all variables and possible references in this namespace' '⎕IO←0' 'r←{⍵,[0.5]⍎¨⍵}'' ''~¨⍨↓⎕NL 2 9'
 r.checkErrorFolder←1
 r.createHTML←1
 r.customFns←''
 r.customFnsParent←⍬
 r.enforceOff←0
 r.errorFolder←''
 r.killThreads←1
 r.logFunction←''
 r.logFunctionParent←⍬
 r.off←1
 r.returnCode←1
 r.saveCrash←1
 r.saveErrorWS←1
 r.saveVars←1
 r.signal←0
 r.stormCeiling←10
 r.stormGuard←0
 r.stormRepeatAfter←24
 r.stormResetAfter←24
 r.trapInternalErrors←1
 r.trapSaveWSID←1
 r.windowsEventSource←''
 r.addToMsg←''
⍝Done
